# Build-time configuration for the CHROME EXTENSION itself.
#
# Separate from `.env.example`, which documents the optional backend service.
# That file says plainly that nothing in it is needed to build the extension,
# and that is still true — this is the file for the two values that are.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED. `npm ci && npm test && npm run build` works with
# none of it set, and that is the configuration every published build has so
# far shipped with. Each value switches on an optional path, and a build
# without it reports that path as unavailable *with a reason* rather than
# offering a control that cannot work.
#
# NO SECRET BELONGS IN THIS FILE, and none of these is one. Values here are
# inlined into `dist/` by Vite at build time, so anyone who unzips the
# extension can read them. A value that must stay confidential cannot be
# configured this way at all — which is why the Google provider authorization
# uses a Chrome Extension OAuth client, the one client type Google issues
# without a secret.
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored; this file and `.env.example` are the two
# exceptions, so the set is discoverable without any value being present.
#
# To use one: copy to `.env`, fill the value, and rebuild. Verify with
# `node scripts/check-extension-env.mjs`, which the build runs for you and
# which states exactly what it decided.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connecting a Google account to use the Gemini API. See
# docs/release/OWNER-CHECKLIST.md section G-6 for the registration steps and
# docs/account-integration.md for what the connection does and does not do.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The OAuth client id, from a Google Cloud OAuth client of application type
# **Chrome Extension**. Ends `.apps.googleusercontent.com`. Public, and paired
# with no secret — Google states a client secret "is not applicable to requests
# from clients registered as Android, iOS, or Chrome applications".
#
# Google pins the client to ONE extension id. An unpacked build and a published
# one have different ids, so a client registered for one will be refused for the
# other — the extension reports that as a build configuration problem rather
# than blaming the user's Google account.
#
# Absent: the panel reports the Google option unavailable, says why, and offers
# the Gemini API key path. Malformed: the build FAILS rather than silently
# behaving as though it were absent.
VITE_ABA_GOOGLE_PROVIDER_CLIENT_ID=

# The Google Cloud project id or number that OAuth-authorized Gemini calls are
# metered and billed against, sent as `x-goog-user-project`.
#
# Google documents that a user-credential call to a client-based API must name
# a project for billing and quota, and answers one that does not with a message
# saying exactly that. This is NOT derived from the client id: the digits at the
# start of a client id usually are a project number, and "usually" is not a
# documented mapping, and this value decides whose quota is spent.
#
# Absent: the header is not sent. The call either works or returns Google's own
# quota-project message, which the extension turns into a sentence naming this
# setting. Set it to the project you enabled the Generative Language API on.
VITE_ABA_GOOGLE_QUOTA_PROJECT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The OPTIONAL product sign-in. Needs a deployed backend; see `.env.example`
# for the backend's own variables and docs/release/OWNER-CHECKLIST.md G-1…G-5.
#
# This is a different thing from the Google connection above: it authorizes AI
# Browser Agent, grants access to no AI service, and is not required to use the
# extension. docs/account-integration.md has the comparison.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The https origin the backend is reachable at, origin only. Absent: the
# extension has no sign-in and says so, which is the state every published
# build has shipped in.
VITE_ABA_BACKEND_ORIGIN=
